card.statement:
In the case of $\mathfrak{gl}(3)$ we can give the following interpretation of the basis elements of the module $V(T(\bar{v}))$. Let $T(v)$ be a generic tableau such that $V(T(v))$ is simple, and let $T(\bar{v})$ be such that $\bar{v}_{21} = \bar{v}_{22}$:

\begin{center}
\Stone{$v_{31}$}\Stone{$v_{32}$}\Stone{$v_{33}$} \Stone{$\bar{v}_{31}$}\Stone{$\bar{v}_{32}$}\Stone{$\bar{v}_{33}$}
[0.2pt]
$T(v)$= \Stone{$v_{21}$}\Stone{$v_{22}$} $T(\bar{v})$
= \Stone{$\bar{v}_{21}$}\Stone{$\bar{v}_{22}$}
[0.2pt]
\Stone{$v_{11}$} \Stone{$\bar{v}_{11}.$}

\end{center}
Then $T(\bar{v}+(m,n,k))$ and $\mathcal{D}T(\bar{v}+(m,n,k))$ can be considered as formal limits in the following way:

\begin{equation}
T(\bar{v}+(m,n,k)):=\lim\limits_{v\to \bar{v}}T(v+(m,n,k)),
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{D}T(\bar{v}+(m,n,k)):=\lim\limits_{v\to \bar{v}}\left(\frac{T(v+(m,n,k))-T(v+(n,m,k))}{v_{21}-v_{22}}\right).
\end{equation}
